      "source": [
        "The K-distance plot shows the point where the distance between neighbors starts to increase sharply. This \"knee\" point is the ideal epsilon. By using this value, I ensure that DBSCAN groups customers who are truly close to each other, while labeling isolated points as noise (Cluster -1).\n",
        "Unlike KMeans, DBSCAN identifies clusters based on density and can detect noise (outliers). This is particularly useful in financial datasets where extreme behaviors may represent niche but valuable segments.\n",
        "The presence of noise points suggests that some customers do not belong to any clear segment, which may indicate irregular or unpredictable behavior patterns.\n",
        "After applying DBSCAN, I observed that the algorithm did not produce clearly defined clusters compared to KMeans, and a portion of the data was classified as noise. This suggests that the dataset does not have strong density-based structures, and that centroid-based methods like KMeans are more suitable for this problem."
